      <text xml:space="preserve">{{Infobox Pope|
English name=Sergius II|
image=[[Image:Vatican coa.png|150px]]|
birth_name=???|
term_start=[[844]]|
term_end=[[847]]|
predecessor=[[Pope Gregory IV|Gregory IV]]|
successor=[[Pope Leo IV|Saint Leo IV]]|
birth_date=???|
birthplace=[[Rome]], [[Italy]]|
dead=dead|death_date=[[847]]|
deathplace=???|}}


'''Sergius II''', was [[Pope]] from [[844]]-[[847]].  A [[Rome|Roman]] of noble birth, elected by the clergy and people to succeed [[Pope Gregory IV|Gregory IV]], he was forthwith consecrated without waiting for the sanction of the emperor [[Lothair I|Lothar]], who accordingly sent his son [[Louis II, Holy Roman Emperor|Louis]] with an army to punish the breach of faith. A pacific arrangement was ultimately made, and Louis was crowned king of [[Lombardy]] by Sergius. In this pontificate Rome was ravaged, and the churches of [[St. Peter's Basilica|St. Peter]] and [[Basilica of Saint Paul Outside the Walls|St. Paul]] were robbed by [[Saracens]], who also attacked [[Porto]] and [[Ostia]] (August [[846]]). Sergius died while negotiating between two patriarchs and was succeeded by [[Pope Leo IV]].

On the death of [[Gregory IV]] the archdeacon [[John]] was proclaimed pope by popular [[acclamation]], while the nobility elected Sergius, and the opposition was suppressed, with Sergius intervening to save John's life. Sergius was then consecrated immediately, without seeking the ratification of the Frankish court.
[[Lothair II of Lotharingia|Lothair II]] disapproved of this abandoning of the [[Constitution]] of [[824]], sending an army under his son Louis, the recently appointed [[viceroy]] of Italy.  An accommodation was, however, reached, with Louis being crowned king of [[Lombardy]] by Sergius. The Pope, however, did not accede to all the demands made upon him.

Sergius went in for much rebuilding of [[Rome]], relying on somewhat dubious means of gaining money. [[Simony]] flourished in his reign.

''From the 9th edition ([[1886]]) of an unnamed encyclopedia''.
