    <title>Porsche 968</title>
    <id>24376</id>
    <revision>
      <id>40028532</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-17T16:38:47Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Sfoskett</username>
        <id>65454</id>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{Infobox Automobile
|image=[[Image:968.JPG|250px|Porsche 968]]
|name=Porsche 968
|manufacturer=[[Porsche]]
|class=[[Sports car]]
|production=[[1992]]&amp;ndash;[[1995]]
|predecessor=[[Porsche 944]]
|successor=[[Porsche Boxster]]&lt;br&gt;[[Porsche Cayman]]
|body_style=2-door [[coupe]]&lt;br&gt;2-door [[convertible]]
|engine=3.0 L [[Straight-4|I4]]
|transmission=6-speed [[manual transmission|manual]]
|wheelbase=94.5&amp;nbsp;in (2400&amp;nbsp;mm)
|length=170.1&amp;nbsp;in (4321&amp;nbsp;mm)
|width=68.3&amp;nbsp;in (1735&amp;nbsp;mm)
|height=50.2&amp;nbsp;in (1275&amp;nbsp;mm)
|weight=3154&amp;nbsp;lb (1431&amp;nbsp;kg)
|fuel_economy=17&amp;nbsp;mpg (13.8&amp;nbsp;L/100&amp;nbsp;km)&lt;br&gt;26&amp;nbsp;mpg (9&amp;nbsp;L/100&amp;nbsp;km)
}}
The '''968''' was an [[automobile]] made by [[Porsche]] [[AG]] of [[Germany]] between 1992 and 1995.  The 968 replaced the [[Porsche 944]].

The 968 actually started development as a 944 &quot;S3&quot;, set to replace the &quot;S2&quot; variant that Porsche had introduced in [[1989]]. However, so many of the 944's components were modified or replaced entirely during development stages that Porsche chose to introduce the variant as a new model, hence the 968 nameplate. It was powered by an updated version of the 944's [[straight-4]] [[internal-combustion engine|engine]], now displacing 3.0&amp;nbsp;L and producing 240&amp;nbsp;[[Horsepower#PS|PS]] (236&amp;nbsp;[[Horsepower#hp (SAE)|hp]]/177&amp;nbsp;[[Watt|kW]]). The 968's engine was the second-largest 4-cylinder ever offered in a production car, the most powerful naturally-aspirated 4-cylinder engine ever sold in a production automobile and with a specific output of around 80&amp;nbsp;hp (60&amp;nbsp;kW) per litre, it had one of the highest power per litre ratings of any street-legal naturally-aspirated automobile ever sold behind only the [[Acura]] ([[Honda]]) [[Honda NSX| NSX]].  This high specific output was partly due to Porsche's VarioCam camshaft phasing device, introduced on this model.

[[Image:Porsche_968_CS.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Porsche 968 CS]]

The 968's styling somewhat resembled the [[Porsche 928|928]] although it looks considerably more like a blend of the [[Porsche 993| Type 993]] [[Porsche 911|911]], which did not appear until the end of the 968's production and a [[Porsche 944| 944 S2]]. Like the 944, the 968 was sold as both a [[coupe]] and a [[convertible]]. Much of the 968's chassis was carried over from the 944S2, which in itself shared many components with the 944 Turbo (internally numbered ''951''). Borrowed components include the [[Brembo]]-sourced 4-piston brake calipers on all four wheels, aluminum semi-trailing arms and aluminum front A-arms, used in a Macpherson strut arrangement. The steel unibody structure was also very similar to the previous models. Porsche, however, maintained that the 968 was more than 80% new in total, which merited it having a new nameplate rather than being a 944 S3 as had been planned.

[[Image:Porsche 968 1.jpg|thumb|left|250px|1994 Porsche 968 cabriolet]]

A street-legal race variant, dubbed '''Club Sport''', appeared later on with a stripped-out interior including racing seats, revised suspension and larger wheels and tires, but was offered only in Europe.  A UK-only version called '''968 Sport''', a Club Sport model with some &quot;comfort&quot; features added back in, was produced in 1994 and 1995.  Porsche briefly produced a [[turbocharger|turbocharged]] version called '''Turbo S'''&amp;mdash;15 were produced in total, and again the car was only sold in Europe.  The 968 Turbo S was capable (in 1993) of 0&amp;ndash;60&amp;nbsp;mph in 4.7&amp;nbsp;s and top speeds approaching 180&amp;nbsp;mph (290&amp;nbsp;km/h).  A race variant called '''968 Turbo RS''' was added as well; only 4 were produced.

There were approximately 2000 cabriolets and 2000 coupes imported to USA 1992&amp;ndash;1995.  Values are steady and appear to have increased in the last 2 years by approx $1000 across the board.   Low milage examples are worth around US$30,000 and 100,000 mile units are worth $11,000&amp;ndash;$15,000.

The 968 was Porsche's last new front-engined vehicle before the introduction of the [[Porsche Cayenne|Cayenne]] SUV in 2003.  It was sold alongside the [[Porsche 928|928]] GTS through 1995 when both models were dropped.  As of 2005, Porsche has not yet built another front-engined sports car.

