  <page>
    <title>Porsche 912</title>
    <id>24377</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41098497</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-25T01:03:43Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Matt Gies</username>
        <id>37551</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>/* Information */</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">The '''Porsche 912''' was a [[sports car]] [[automobile]] manufactured by [[Porsche]] GmbH of [[Germany]] between [[1965]] and [[1969]] as their entry-level model.

== Information ==
{| border=1 align=&quot;right&quot; cellpadding=2 cellspacing=0 width=250 style=&quot;margin-left:3em; margin-bottom: 2em;&quot;
|-
|colspan=2|[[Image:Porsche 912.jpg|250px|Porsche 912]]
|-
!colspan=2 style=&quot;color: white; background: darkgreen;&quot;|Porsche 912
|-
|Manufacturer:||[[Porsche]]
|-
|Class:||[[RR layout|RR]] [[coupe]]
|-
|Production:||[[1965]] &amp;ndash; [[1969]]
|-
|Successor:||[[Porsche 914]]
|-
|Body Styles:||[[Coupe]]
|-
|Engines:||1.6 L, 2.0 L [[flat-4]]
|}

After the discontinuation of the [[Porsche 356|356]] model in [[1965]], Porsche was left with the pricey new [[Porsche 911|911]] as their only offering. Fearing that its considerable price increase over the 356 would cost the company sales and narrow appeal to the brand, a decision was made by executives to introduce a new entry-level model. Built on the 911's chassis and sharing its bodyshell, Porsche was able to offer the 912 for much less than an entry level 911 by using the four-cylinder engine from the 356 rather than the 911's &quot;flat&quot; six-cylinder powerplant and by reducing the number of standard features. The proven reliability of the 356's powerplant combined with the 911 bodywork and low price made the 912 a very attractive buy to both new and old customers, and it substantially outsold the 911 during the first few years of production; Porsche produced slightly more than 30,000 units during it's five year production run. 

After updating the 911 lineup to include both a more powerful 911S and a less expensive 911T, Porsche excutives began to feel that the 912 had become redundant, that the 911 platform was sufficently diverse and that pricing had largely come into line with market expectations. Owing to this and the desire to introduce a new model, the 912 was discontinued and replaced as Porsche's entry-level model by the [[Porsche 914|914]] in [[1970]], a vehicle which Porsche had thought would be less expensive for them to manufacture and sell than the 912. 

After a six year absence, the model was re-introduced to North America in [[1976]] as the 912E to occupy the entry-level position left vacant by the discontinuation of the 914 while the new [[Porsche 924|924]], the 914's offical replacment, was being finalized and put into production. It featured the &quot;G-Series&quot; 911 bodywork and was powered by Volkwagen's 2.0 litre [[Type IV]] four-cylinder engine, previously used in late-model versions of the 914/4. 2,099 were manufactured in total, and were not offically sold outside the [[United States|U.S.]].

==External links==
* [http://www.912registry.org/history.htm 912 Registry Site: 912 Heritage]
* [http://www.912register.co.uk 912 Register UK]

[[Category:Porsche vehicles|912]]
[[Category:Sports cars]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
