      <minor />
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{Infobox Pope|
English name=Victor I|
image=[[Image:Victor I.jpg|150px]]|
birth_name=???|
term_start=[[189]]|
term_end=[[199]]|
predecessor=[[Pope Eleuterus|Saint Eleuterus]]|
successor=[[Pope Zephyrinus|Saint Zephyrinus]]|
birth_date=???|
birthplace=[[Africa (province)|province of Africa]]|
dead=dead|death_date=[[199]]|
deathplace=[[Rome]], [[Italy]]|}}

'''Saint Victor I''' was Bishop of Rome (now called [[pope]]) from [[189]] to [[199]] (the Vatican cites [[186]] or [[189]] to [[197]] or [[201]]). He was the first &quot;African&quot; pope having born in the [[Africa (province)|province of Africa]], and later came to [[Rome]]. He was later [[canonization|canonized]].

Until his pontificate, a difference in dating the celebration of the [[Christian Passover]]/[[Easter]] between Rome and the bishops of [[Asia Minor]] had been tolerated. The churches in Asia Minor celebrated it on the [[Quartodeciman|14th of the Jewish month of Nisan]], the Jewish [[Passover]], regardless of what day it falls, as the Crucifixion had occurred on the Friday before Passover. The Latins called them ''Quartodecimans'' (see article [[Quartodecimanism]]). Rome and the West celebrated Easter on the Sunday following the 14th of Nisan. Victor is remembered for the intolerance he displayed towards any lack of uniformity in the church by excommunicating the bishops such as [[Polycrates of Ephesus]] who opposed his views on Easter. He also excommunicated [[Theodotus of Byzantium]] for his [[Christology|beliefs about Christ]].

Until Victor's time, Rome celebrated the [[Mass (liturgy)|Mass]] in [[Greek language|Greek]]. Pope Victor changed the language to [[Latin]], which was used in his native North Africa. According to [[Jerome]], he was the first  Christian author to write about theology in Latin.


