    <title>Pope Innocent VI</title>
    <id>24473</id>
    <revision>
      <id>39854213</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-16T08:35:16Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>153.1.14.116</ip>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{Infobox Pope|
English name=Innocent VI|
image=[[Image:Innozenz VI.gif|150px]]|
birth_name=Étienne Aubert|
term_start=[[December 18]], [[1352]]|
term_end=[[September 12]], [[1362]]|
predecessor=[[Pope Clement VI|Clement VI]]|
successor=[[Pope Urban V|Urban V]]|
birth_date=[[1282]] or [[1295]]|
birthplace= [[Beyssac]], [[France]] |
dead=dead|death_date=[[September 12]], [[1362]]|
deathplace=[[Avignon]], [[France]]|}}

'''Innocent VI''', né '''''Étienne Aubert''''' ([[1282]] or [[1295]] &amp;ndash; [[September 12]], [[1362]]), [[Pope]] at [[Avignon Papacy|Avignon]] from [[1352]] to [[1362]], the successor of [[Pope Clement VI]] (1342&amp;ndash;52), was a native of the hamlet of Les Monts, [[diocese]] of [[Limoges]] (today part of the [[commune in France|commune]] of [[Beyssac]], ''[[département in France|département]]'' of [[Corrèze]]), and, after having taught [[civil law]] at [[Toulouse]], became bishop successively of [[Noyon]] and of [[Clermont]]. 

In [[1342]], he was raised to the position of [[Cardinal (Catholicism)|cardinal]]. On the death of Clement VI, after the cardinals had each bound themselves to a particular line of policy should he be elected, Aubert was chosen ([[December 18]], 1352), taking the name of Innocent VI; one of the first acts of his pontificate was to declare the pact to have been illegal and null. 

His subsequent policy compares favourably with that of the other [[Avignon Papacy|Avignon Popes]]. He brought about many needed reforms in the administration of church affairs, and by his legate, Cardinal [[Gil Alvarez De Albornoz|Albornoz]], who was accompanied by [[Cola di Rienzi|Rienzi]], he sought to restore order in [[Rome]], where, in [[1355]], [[Charles IV, Holy Roman Emperor|Charles IV]] (1346&amp;ndash;78) was with his permission crowned, after previously having come under an oath that he would quit the city on the day of the ceremony.

It was largely through the exertions of Innocent VI that the [[Treaty of Brétigny]] ([[1360]]) between [[France]] and [[England]] was brought about. During his pontificate, [[John V Palaeologus]] (1341&amp;ndash;47, 1354&amp;ndash;76, 1379&amp;ndash;90, 1390&amp;ndash;91) offered to submit the [[Greece|Greek]] church to the [[Holy See|Roman See]] on condition of assistance being rendered against [[John VI Cantacuzenus]] (1347&amp;ndash;54). The resources at the disposal of the Pope, however, were all required for exigencies nearer home, and the offer was declined. 

Innocent VI was a liberal patron of letters, and, if the extreme severity of his measures against the [[Fraticelli]] are ignored, he retains a high reputation for justice and mercy. He died on September 12, 1362, and his successor was [[Pope Urban V]] (1362&amp;ndash;70). Today his tomb can be found in the [[Carthusian]] monastery of [[Villeneuve-les-Avignon]].
 
See also: list of [[Pope Innocent|Popes named Innocent]]

----

''Modified text from the 9th edition (1879) of an unnamed encyclopedia''

