  <page>
    <title>Pants</title>
    <id>24500</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41818653</id>
      <timestamp>2006-03-01T23:15:35Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Naconkantari</username>
        <id>676502</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>Reverted edits by [[Special:Contributions/Oilybaldman|Oilybaldman]] ([[User talk:Oilybaldman|talk]]) to last version by FireFox</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{wiktionary}}
'''Pants''' are [[clothing]] for the body below the waist, a must for modesty regardless of gender; the term is an abbreviation of pantaloons, a puzzling [[plurale tantum]] as it comes from the French ''pantalon'', stemming via the buffoonish [[commedia dell'arte]]-character Pantalone from that Italian word.

*In British English, the term '''pants''' refers to '''[[underpants]]'''. It is a catch-all term that can denote anything from [[thong_(garment)|thongs]] to [[boxer shorts]], but all have the common feature that they are worn under trousers, skirts or pyjamas.  The word &quot;pants&quot; has acquired humorous connotations in the last few decades, largely among younger people. Many [[stand-up comedy|stand-up comedians]] have used the word as a light [[pejorative]] term. There is a tendency to use it as an adjective. This usage gained wider attention when it came to light that the then-[[Prime Minister]] [[John Major]] tucked his shirt into his underpants.

*In [[Canadian English|Canadian]], [[Australian English|Australian]] and [[American English]], however, the term '''pants''' refers to a long outer garment worn over the hips and legs, which in British English are called '''[[trousers]]'''.

*In [[American English|American]] [[slang]], the expression &quot;to '''pants''' someone&quot; means to pull a person's trousers down as a sophomoric [[prank]], known as [[list of school pranks#Keg| (de)pantsing]].

*Term used to describe a situation that is worse than bad.

*In Canadian drug use slang, &quot;pants&quot; can sometimes be a codeword for [[heroin]].

*In some cases, this is also a term of endearment for one's younger brother.

{{disambig}}

[[ja:パンツ]]
[[zh:%E8%A4%B2]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Progressive music</title>
    <id>24501</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41557316</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-28T03:23:55Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Yvwv</username>
        <id>427828</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>/* See also */</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Progressive music''' is the name given to a certain approach to [[musical composition]] that has been applied to several different [[music genres]]. One way the term has been applied is to subgenres that have evolved from their root genre by innovating, either through incorporating instruments from other genres or using new techniques within the framework provided by the instrumentation of the root genre to make a new or [[Crossover (music)|crossover]] style. Another is in reference to a gradual build-up of [[energy]] within progressive music track or throughout an [[album]].

==History==
The description &quot;progressive&quot; was first applied to [[jazz]]. The term, coined by [[Stan Kenton]], was in reference to [[cool jazz]] tracks that used instrumentation create an almost [[art music]] like sound. The genre is closely related to [[bebop]].

==Progressive rock==
{{main|Progressive rock}}

In [[Rock and roll|rock]], the word usually describes music that expands traditional musical structures. Progressive rock artists often string together the songs so that the entire album will become an uninterrupted musical &quot;journey&quot;. Each track can be divided into separate movements, all with its own place in the overarching theme of the song. Each movement occasionally has its own name, as in the title track from [[Pink Floyd]]'s ''[[Atom Heart Mother]]''. Progressive rock tends to be very [[melody|melodic]]. Progressive rock's popularity peaked in the mid 1970s with bands such as [[Yes (band)|Yes]], [[Genesis (band)|Genesis]] and [[Emerson, Lake &amp;amp; Palmer]].

===Examples of progressive rock artists===

*[[The Mars Volta]]
*[[The Fall of Troy]]
*[[Coheed and Cambria]]
*[[Pink Floyd]]
*[[King Crimson]]
*[[Marillion]]
*[[Procol Harum]]
*[[Jethro Tull]]
*[[Genesis (band)|Genesis]]
*[[Emerson, Lake &amp; Palmer]]
*[[Frank Zappa]]
*[[Yes (band)|Yes]]
*[[Rush (band)|Rush]]
*[[Soft Machine]]
*[[Dream Theater]]
*[[Gentle Giant]]

==Progressive metal==
{{main|Progressive metal}}

Progressive metal emerged in the 1980s with thrash bands such as [[Diamond Head (group)|Diamond Head]] (''Lightning to the Nations''), [[Metallica]] (''...And Justice For All'') and [[Megadeth]] (''Rust In Peace''), who brought complicated guitar compositions, time changes, and longer songs to [[heavy metal music|heavy metal]]. Combined with traditional sounds of the 70s movement of prog rock, progressive metal has evolved through the work of artists like [[Dream Theater]], [[Opeth]], [[Queensrÿche]], [[Stratovarius]], [[Symphony X]], and [[Tool (band)|Tool]].

==Progressive dance music==
{{main|Progressive dance music}}

Progressive dance music styles include the progressive subgenres of [[house music|house]], [[techno music|techno]], [[trance music|trance]] and [[breakbeat]] (also known as ''breaks''). The use of the term here applies to both the use of new instrumentation within existing genres to create a new sound and the use of [[arrangement]] and [[dynamics (music)|dynamics]] to subtly (unlike with genres such as [[house music|hard house]] or [[Hi-NRG]]) build up to peaks within the track, something used creatively by [[DJs]] who [[beatmatch]] to buildup energy on the dance floor when playing to a crowd.

==See also==
* [[Art rock]]
* [[Progressive bluegrass]]
* [[Progressive country]]
* [[Avant-progressive rock]]
* [[Chord progression]]
* [[Concept album]]
* [[Electronic art music]]
* [[Progg]] and [[progg music]] - a Swedish cultural movement not to be confused with progressive music

==External links==
*[http://www.somethingprog.com Somethingprog]
*[http://www.progressia.net Progressia]
*[http://melosprogbazaar.com Progressive Rock Forum - Melo's Prog Bazaar]
*[http://www.progressivehouse.com Progressivehouse.com] A popular portal for progressive house producers, DJ's and listeners.
*[http://www.progressiveears.com Progressive Ears] A stimulating forum-based progressive rock website, with user-contributed reviews and a wealth of resources.

[[Category:Musical genres]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
